          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Samuel Taylor Coleridge's Early Life</strong><br>\nSamuel Taylor Coleridge was born in 1772 in Ottery St Mary, Devonshire. He was the youngest of ten children in a family rooted in the church; his father was a vicar and a schoolmaster. A dreamy and imaginative child, Coleridge read extensively, which laid the groundwork for his future literary endeavors.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Education and Early Writing</strong><br>\nColeridge's education at Christ's Hospital and Jesus College, Cambridge, did not lead to a degree, as he struggled with debt and depression. His early poetry, such as \"Monody on the Death of Chatterton,\" shows an affinity for both the natural and the supernatural, themes he would explore throughout his career.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Founding the Romantic Movement</strong><br>\nAlong with William Wordsworth, Coleridge helped establish the Romantic movement in England. Their joint work, \"Lyrical Ballads,\" published in 1798, is considered a seminal collection that defined the era's poetic diction and themes, emphasizing emotion and nature over the classical and formal.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
