          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>\"The Rime of the Ancient Mariner\"</strong><br>\nColeridge's best-known work, \"The Rime of the Ancient Mariner,\" is a long poem with gothic elements that tells of a sailor's experiences at sea. The poem is renowned for its rich imagery, moral complexity, and exploration of man's relationship with the natural world and the divine.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Literary Criticism and Philosophy</strong><br>\nApart from poetry, Coleridge was an influential literary critic and philosopher. His lectures and writings, particularly \"Biographia Literaria,\" significantly shaped the understanding and critique of literature. His philosophical explorations included the theory of imagination and the unities of Shakespeare's plays.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Opium Addiction and Impact on Work</strong><br>\nColeridge's struggle with opium addiction deeply affected his personal and professional life. It began as self-medication for rheumatic pain and grew into a dependency that plagued him for most of his adult life, influencing both the visionary quality and the erratic production of his work.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
