          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Friendship with Wordsworth</strong><br>\nThe friendship between Coleridge and Wordsworth was both profound and tumultuous. While their collaboration was productive, their relationship strained due to personal and philosophical differences, as well as Coleridge's issues with opium. Despite this, they remained important to each other's work and legacies.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Life and Lectures</strong><br>\nIn his later years, Coleridge gave a series of influential lectures in London on literature and philosophy. Despite his waning health and ongoing addiction, these lectures enhanced his reputation as a thinker and critic. He also took refuge in the home of Dr. James Gillman, where he continued his writing and intellectual endeavors.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Coleridge's Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nColeridge died in 1834, but his legacy as a poet, critic, and philosopher endures. His work continues to be studied for its poetic innovation, depth of insight, and contribution to English Romanticism. Coleridge's influence on literature, criticism, and philosophy remains profound, marking him as one of the most important figures in English literature.</p>\n"
              },
